   base 64 is just an encoding and not an encryption

	differenece between encoding--- encrypting---- and hash function

	   encoding
	   ````````it is only afunction that is always possible to reverse somehow
		  it might or might not require a secret
		  it can just be a mathematical transformation that doesn't even need
		  a secret to be known to revert it

		e.g. string 1234 => 4321   (one kind of encoding) rule is read from left to right
		cypher was used like mapping alphabet to other alphabet

	  encryption
	  ```````````we are transforming an input to an output
		    but to go back from output to input we always need to have some secret
		we can say that it is a particular way of encoding for which we always need a secret
		encryption is a transformation that assures us that not everyone can find input from an output

	  hash function
	  ``````````````
		from an input we can alwasy get an output
		but we can never by any means find the input from the output
		two rule of hash function
		1) o ---x---> i 
		2) i -- -- -->
		given an input we can tell for certain that it corresponds to the output

		hash functions are the preferred way to store temporary or permanent password
		 
		md5> it's a hash transformation but is not recommended 
			it was proved to be mathematically possible to reach input from the output
			always from the same input it will have same ouptut which leads to rainbow tables

		at present we don't use a hash function which always produces the same output for same input
		we use whcih provide salting which creates different outpout for same input but privides flexibility 
		to compare the output

		HttpBasic is always base64 encoded that's why we should never use HttpBasic outside https
		
